[{"id":110929,"sender_id":842,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Huan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c24a7269ec5dbfcdb95099be6f0ae240?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Geometric.20version.20of.20Red-shift.20conjecture/near/110929\">#1</a> I have a maybe naive question. Is there a geometric interpretation of Redshift conjecture? For instance, should a n-vector bundle over a \"space\" be equivalent to a (n-1)-vector bundle over a suitable loop space of it?</p>","timestamp":1694136920,"subject":"Geometric version of Red-shift conjecture","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":110930,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Geometric.20version.20of.20Red-shift.20conjecture/near/110930\">#2</a> Yes, but it’s subtle: </p>\n<p>First, typically it is fairly immediate that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-bundles on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> transgress to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>-bundles on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span>. </p>\n<p>(This is completely formal for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-bundles with structure <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-group a discrete <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{B}^{n-1} A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> – as in <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2212.13836.pdf#page=5\">pp. 5 here</a> – but with some massaging the transgression process typically works more generally.)</p>\n<p>But what is more subtle is to characterize the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>-bundles on loop spaces <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> that appear as transgressions of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span>-bundles this way: These carry some extra structure and it is only with this extra structure that they will be equivalent to their “de-transgressions” on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>The historically most studied case is that of String 2-bundles: As you know, before these were even defined, Witten had essentially thought of them as 1-bundles on loop space. Then in <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/What%2Bis%2Ban%2Belliptic%2Bobject%253F\">What is an elliptic object?</a></em> and in <em>The spinor bundle on loop space</em> (<a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Stolz-Teichner-SpinorOnLoopSpace.pdf\">pdf</a>) Stolz et al. had sketched out how these “Spin” 1-bundles on loop space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> ought to be equivalent to String 2-bundles on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>It was only much later, namely just recently, that these ideas have been substantiated and proven by Konrad Waldorf et al. First, Konrad pinpointed the extra structure on the transgression of a String 2-bundle to a 1-bundle on loop space, he calls it “<a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/fusion%20bundle\">fusion structure</a>”. The required equivalence was then essentially proven, I think, in <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.09797\">arXiv:2206.09797</a>, see at <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/stringor%20bundle\">stringor bundle</a></em>.</p>\n<p>It is hence no less than 36 years after Witten’s “The Index Of The Dirac Operator In Loop Space” that this transgression/de-transgression relation between String 2-bundles and Spinor 1-bundles on loop space has been really understood. </p>\n<p>What does remain far less understood (as far as I am aware) is the closer relation of any of this to iterated algebraic K-theory and hence to the redshift conjecture. I think all we really have in hands is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>the relation between String-structure and elliptic cohomology via the <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/string%20orientation%20of%20tmf\">string orientation of tmf</a>,</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>the argument that <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/BDR%202-vector%20bundles\">BDR 2-vector bundles</a> are classified by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(\\mathrm{ku})</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">K</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">ku</span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> and as such constitute a “form of” elliptic cohomology (for low values of “form”, I suppose).</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>While suggestive, the concrete relation between these two items remains rather vague, as far as I am aware.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, while the redshift conjecture is somewhat reminiscent of what happens in the now well-understood case of String 2-bundles transgressing to 1-bundles on loop space, substantial details on the relation remain scarce.</p>","timestamp":1694158160,"subject":"Geometric version of Red-shift conjecture","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]},{"id":110939,"sender_id":842,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Huan","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c24a7269ec5dbfcdb95099be6f0ae240?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/5-nLab-General-Discussions/topic/Geometric.20version.20of.20Red-shift.20conjecture/near/110939\">#3</a> Thank you very much, Urs.</p>","timestamp":1694172972,"subject":"Geometric version of Red-shift conjecture","stream_id":5,"display_recipient":"nLab General Discussions","reactions":[]}]